WiiStation CHANGE LOG:
Next version (not released yet):
* PSX Pad Type Analog is ready to play: a game that asks the DualShock about its
  modes and leaves the choice to the player (Gex 3, MediEvil) now gets analog mode
  (red light) by itself after 10 seconds, so the left stick works without the ANALOG key
  combination. Before, the stick did nothing in those games until the combination
  was pressed. The combination still switches back to digital, a game's own
  choice still wins, and digital-only games stay digital. New settings.ini key
  AnalogAuto: 0 = as the real pad, 1 = this (the default), 2 = every game starts
  in analog mode.

Ver 5.19.0 (2026-10-28):
* Achievements page: the Account row is gone (accounts are in Players), and the
  notes say what each mode does: Local and Official use the RetroAchievements
  set; Custom and Test run your own set file.
* The General page's Cheevos button is now Achievements, everywhere.
* The Achievements settings page: the buttons no longer touch each other or
  the notes under them.
* In the Achievements book, the menu combination (the one that opens the menu
  in a game) goes straight back into the game.
* B on the main menu goes back into the game when one has been started (with
  no game started it does nothing, as before).
* The book names the Mii that sits on the seat now: it asks the seats again
  when it opens (a Mii changed in Players showed the old name until the game
  went on), and in Official mode with no other Mii it names the Mii that plays
  Official instead of Guest.
* Players: every Mii seated on a port with a connected pad earns its own
  achievements (Custom and Local modes). The toast says who: "Kat unlocked", "Kat and
  Ollie unlocked", "4 players unlocked". An achievement one Mii has keeps
  working for the others who do not have it yet. With no Mii seated, Guest
  earns, as before; every unlock earned until now stays with Guest.
* The Achievements page shows one Mii at a time, with its coin and count; L
  and R (+ and - on a Wii Remote) switch.
* The Achievements page is a book: a header with the player's coin and name,
  the game and the total ("12 / 89 earned", "95 / 610 points", a bar); tabs In
  progress, Locked and Earned (Left and Right; Leaderboards is a fourth tab
  in Official mode); big rows with the badge, description, points and, for a
  measured achievement, a bar with its count. A opens an achievement: a large
  badge, description, progress, points, rarity, type, when it was earned and
  the picture taken when it unlocked. B goes back.
* An unlock saves the game's frame to wiistation/achievements/shots/<game>/
  <achievement>.png, once per achievement; the card is not in it. The file is
  made in small slices, so the game does not stop for it. The book shows it in
  the achievement's detail.
* Open the menu while an unlock card, the progress panel or a challenge icon is
  on screen and the book opens on that achievement.
* The progress panel shows only at each 10% step (10%, 20% ...), never for the
  first reading after loading a game, a disc swap, a reset or a state load,
  and not in the first 3 seconds (the game's memory is not set up yet).
* The unlock card, the progress panel and the challenge icons sit 16 pixels
  from the right edge of the picture (were 40). With Screen Mode Force 16:9 on
  OpenGX they sat 14% of the screen short of it (the game fills the whole
  screen there, but the pop-ups counted from a 4:3 picture's edge); now they
  end at the screen's edge, 16 pixels of a 640 wide picture short of it.
* Small text (the pop-ups, the FPS lines, the menu below full size) is drawn
  from glyphs reduced to the pixels they cover, each on a whole pixel, and no
  longer breaks into dots and gaps. The pop-ups' flat shapes and text are never
  dithered by the GX Color setting.
* The unlock card and the progress panel keep moving while the game shows a
  still picture or a black screen (loading): they used to freeze until the game
  drew again, and the game card at a game's start froze half slid in.
* New setting AchievementChallenge (General, Achievements, Challenge icons): Off
  hides the flashing icons of achievements that are one step away.
* Save states made by other players keep the game, but the unlocks come from
  the Miis seated now: a state never moves one Mii's unlocks to another.
* The unlock journal has a player column: "<set digest> <id> <player key>
  <title>". Lines without a key are Guest's. Official mode is unchanged.
* Players: an Edit slot right of the coins. Drop a Mii on it (or pick it with
  A, then Down to Edit) for its player settings: a RetroAchievements account
  and Official.
* Players: a Mii's account is a file, wiistation/achievements/ra/<name>.ini
  (lines as in ra_account.ini). The file browser lists them by name, with
  "None: earn Local" and "This Wii's account" (ra_account.ini). A linked Mii's
  coin wears an "RA" tag.
* Players: a linked Mii's card has an Official button. The lit one plays
  Official; pressing another moves it there. Port 1's Mii by default. The
  choice is saved.
* Official mode logs in with the account of the Mii that plays Official: its
  file in wiistation/achievements/ra/, or this Wii's ra_account.ini (the
  default, and for a Mii that is not linked). A game load with another account
  than the last one logs out and in again; nothing changes in the middle of a
  game. If that account does not work (no file, a failed login), this Wii's
  account loads the set only to look (nothing is sent to it) and that Mii earns
  Local, with the reason on its Achievements page.
* Official mode with several Miis: the Official Mii's unlocks go to
  RetroAchievements; the other seated Miis earn the same set Local, in
  wiistation/achievements/ra_local/<game id>.unlocks. An achievement the
  Official account already has stays earnable for them (encore mode is on while
  a Local Mii is seated), and nothing is sent twice. The toast has two tags
  over its names: "RetroAchievements" and "Local". The Achievements page lists
  the Official Mii first, then each Local Mii.
* scripts/ra_setup.py: --ra-name NAME with --account FILE puts the file in
  achievements/ra/NAME.ini for a Mii to link.
* New mode Local (Achievements = 4): the official RetroAchievements set for the
  game, and nothing is ever sent to the site. Every player (each seated Mii, or
  Guest) earns into the Local journal, wiistation/achievements/ra_local/<game
  id>.unlocks, with Casual rules; the book, toasts, badges, progress and unlock
  pictures work as in Official. Downloading the set needs a RetroAchievements
  login once (the Official Mii's account, this Wii's ra_account.ini, or the
  first file in achievements/ra/); the set is saved on the card
  (achievements/ra_cache/), so the game loads again with no network and no
  account, and the set is asked for again after 30 days. With no network and
  nothing saved, the game card says so.
* The mode formerly called Local (your own definition file, unlocks kept) is
  now Custom. Settings files keep working: 1 = Custom, 2 = Test, 3 = Official,
  4 = Local. The menu cycles Off, Local, Official, Custom, Test.
* Ape Escape: a scene that turns on like a CRT (the professor's lab, the
  "Capture N monkeys" screen) no longer shows the whole picture for a moment
  before its line widens (OpenGX).
* Far Textures (Settings > Advanced > GPU): Anisotropic 2x and 4x are gone.
  They broke sprites with cut-out edges (Ape Escape's hand cursor lost its
  white inside). Off and Linear stay; a saved 2 or 3 reads as Linear.
Ver 5.18.2 (2026-10-28):
* Players: the seats are in their own file, wiistation/players.ini, written
  when the Players menu closes (no Save Settings needed). Seats saved in
  settings.ini by 5.18.0/5.18.1 are still read.
* Players: the Miis you seated last come first in the list.
* Players: a seated Mii deleted in the Mii Channel becomes Guest, with a
  notice when the Players menu opens; its unlocks are kept.
* Players: Guest and the other centered names sit in the middle of their slot.
* Achievements page: the notes that ran off the bottom of the screen are gone (the
  rest is in SETTINGS.md).
Ver 5.18.1 (2026-10-28):
* Players: a Co-Op port's card holds all its players (up to 8). Open it with
  A, a click on it or its "..." button, or by dropping a coin on it: the same
  screen then shows a card per Co-Op player. B leaves it.
* Players: round coin slots with Guest centered in them, a solid focus ring,
  "Port 1A" under a seated Mii's coin, the socket centered on a port's card,
  names that fit their card, and the page of Miis at the bottom right.
* US spelling throughout the menus and documents (the FmvColour setting keeps
  its name, so settings files still work).
Ver 5.18.0 (2026-10-28):
* Players (Main menu): the Miis on your Wii as players. Each PlayStation port
  is a card (four per port with a multitap); seat a Mii on it by holding A+B
  on its coin with the Wii Remote and dropping it on the card, or with a pad:
  A on a coin, choose the card, A. A favorite Mii's coin has a gold rim.
  Faces need HBC's HOME > HBC > Save Mii faces once; without it the coins show
  initials. The Mii Channel's database is only read, never written. Save the
  settings to keep the seats. Per-player achievements come next.
* Built with devkitPPC r51 (GCC 16.2) and libogc 3.2.1, and the Homebrew
  Channel agent 1.12.1.

Ver 5.17.0 (2026-10-24):
* Achievements, Official mode (Settings > General > Achievements): RetroAchievements
  sets, earned online with your RetroAchievements account (Casual; Hardcore
  needs RetroAchievements' approval first). The disc is identified the way
  RetroAchievements does it, from every format WiiStation boots (CUE/BIN, ISO,
  CCD, MDS, TOC, CBN, CHD, PBP). Setup: Docs/ACHIEVEMENTS.md, "Official login"
  (an account file, a certificate bundle and a random seed on the SD card).
  Unlocks made offline wait and are sent when the connection is back.
* Achievements: an unlock shows a PlayStation-style card for 5 s with the
  title, points, how rare it is, and the set's progress. Its color is the
  tier: Blue, Bronze, Silver, Gold, Crystal (by rarity, or by points in a local
  set). Beating a game and mastering a set get their own cards.
* Achievements: a card with a spinning PlayStation disc when a game's set
  loads (N of M earned, points); a small panel at the bottom right when a
  counted achievement moves on ("12/40"); a box for each achievement that is
  one step away. RetroAchievements badges show in the card, the panel and the
  boxes (saved on the card in achievements/badges).
* Current ISO > Achievements lists each achievement with Earned or how far
  along it is, its points and how rare it is, and what you are doing in the
  game (rich presence). Leaderboards: the ten best scores and yours, for
  each of the game's boards (reading only: RetroAchievements takes scores in
  Hardcore only).
* Achievements settings Unofficial (the set's not yet approved achievements too)
  and Encore (earn your achievements again this session). Save states keep
  achievement progress in Official mode too.
* Official mode needs no clock setting any more: the Wii learns its time zone
  from RetroAchievements' answers. The certificate list comes with the install.
* Achievements and disc swaps: a multi-disc game keeps its set when you swap
  to another of its discs (Official: RetroAchievements checks the disc; local
  sets: Game=SLUS00892,SLUS00908 names every disc). Another game's disc turns
  achievements off until the next game load.
* Achievements, Official mode: the set loads in the menu, before the game
  starts. The login happens when the menu opens, and Play Game waits (with
  "Loading achievements...", at most 8 s) until the set is in, so the game no
  longer stutters in its first seconds while the set is read.
* Achievements: RetroAchievements' Hardcore notice ("Unknown Emulator") is a
  black card of its own, not an unlock.
* Achievements cost less: Official mode now takes about 5 % of a frame in
  Spyro (was 7 %).
* Several games in one session no longer run the Wii out of memory (Crash
  Team Racing after Spyro the Dragon could freeze in a race): the emulator's
  growing memory and the screen buffers moved to the Wii's larger memory.
* Start-up: if WiiStation ever hangs before its menu appears (a card that
  does not answer, for one), it goes back to the Homebrew Channel by itself
  after 90 s and leaves a report there, instead of freezing the Wii.
* Lightrec: a code-cache clear near the top of PS1 RAM no longer writes past
  its table.
* ISO Info is a page of its own (Current ISO > ISO Info): file, label, ID,
  size, tracks, system, automatic fixes and achievements. A PS-X .exe says it
  gets no achievements (it has no disc to identify).
* The game list shows only what WiiStation can load: disc images and PS-X .exe,
  one entry per game (a .cue's track files, an .img beside its .ccd, an .mdf
  beside its .mds are not listed). Covers, notes, .sub and .sbi files are gone.
* Menu buttons fit their text: a label longer than its button is drawn
  smaller (the ANALOG combo on Configure Buttons, 240p and Interlaced on Video).
* Disc images: picking the .mds of an Alcohol 120% image (.mds + .mdf) starts
  the game; it silently did nothing (only the .mdf worked). Images with the
  subchannel stored after each sector (cdrdao .toc RW, .mds with subchannel)
  now read ahead like every other format. CHDs made with large hunks (the
  smallest files) now unpack ahead and play as smoothly as a .cue.
* PBP: every disc of a multi-disc PBP can be played. Swap CD and pick the same
  PBP again to move to its next disc (the message says which). Before, only
  disc 1 was reachable. PBPs made with compression level 0 (stored, as
  PSX2PSP's fastest setting makes them) boot; they never did.
* OpenGX: a still picture the game loads straight into the screen without
  drawing or flipping shows again (Spyro: Year of the Dragon's Universal logo
  was black). A picture loaded long before the game shows it appears too
  (Vagrant Story's title logo was missing behind the menu). FF7's opening
  green frame is gone again (back since 5.12.2).
* OpenGX texture cache: a freed texture's space is reused again (since September 2026 it
  was given back smaller than it took, so the cache filled with unusable
  slivers that every new texture searched). Crash Team Racing's Tiger Temple,
  whose rain makes thousands of tiny textures, runs at full speed in the rain:
  0.88x -> 0.98x over a whole race on the bench Wii (debug build).
* Lightrec compiles new code on a background thread while the game runs it in
  the interpreter, so a level that loads a lot of new code stutters less
  (Spyro's whirlwind elevator: 107 ms lost on the bench Wii before, 7 ms now;
  the time the emulator spends compiling: 1.41 s before, 0.53 s now). The switch
  to compiled code happens at a fixed point in the game's own run, so a game
  and a recording replay exactly the same every time. Recordings made with
  earlier versions can drift in some games (Crash Bandicoot 3); hidden setting
  JitDelay=0 restores the old timing.

Ver 5.16.0 (2026-10-20):
* USB controllers: every button, hat and axis can be used. A USB gamepad with
  no controller ini now works: WiiStation reads its HID report descriptor and
  lays it out as a standard pad (buttons 1-4 Square Cross Circle Triangle, 5-8
  L1 R1 L2 R2, 9 Select, 10 Start, 11 L3, 12 R3, the hat as the D-pad, X/Y and
  Z/Rz or Rx/Ry as the sticks). Old controller inis work as before.
* Controller inis can name any input: KeyL3=Button11, KeyUp=HatUp,
  KeyL2=AxisZ+, StickX=AxisRz, or the old byte,mask (Docs/CONTROLLER_TESTING.md
  section 7). New keys KeyL3 and KeyR3; the DualShock 3/4 and Logitech Dual
  Action / RumblePad 2 inis now have L3 and R3.
* Configure Buttons for a USB pad offers L3, R3 and every input of the pad by
  name (Btn 13, Hat Up, Axis Z+), for any PlayStation button and for the menu,
  fast-forward, screenshot and ANALOG combinations (a Home button can open the
  menu alone). Analog sticks can come from any axis pair. The A-/C- stick
  direction entries now work.
* Test USB Pad shows which HID input each press moved and writes a complete
  suggested controller ini into usbpad.log (scripts/usbpad_read.py --ini).
* Soft Mode (Settings > Video > Advanced, SoftGpuMode): the soft
  renderers (Soft Fast, Soft Timed) draw in one of three ways. Accurate, the
  default, draws as a PS1 GPU: its dither pattern, semi-transparency, the mask
  bit on every write (polygons, rectangles, lines, image loads, VRAM copies),
  the palette and texture caches. Every ps1-tests GPU test with a real-PS1
  result passes with it (gpu/bandwidth also with GPU Timing Accurate). Hi-Fi
  draws the same pixels without the dither, with finer color blends. Fast is
  the old code (5.15.0 and before), the cheapest on the Wii.
* GPU Timing Accurate: with Soft Mode Accurate or Hi-Fi, the GPU stays busy as
  long as each draw takes on a PS1, and image transfers by DMA take PS1 time.
  GPUSTAT bit 28 (ready for a DMA block) is clear while the GPU is busy.
* Filter Tour: no more Deflicker and Trap steps. It now steps through the
  texture filter, Soft Mode and GPU Timing on the soft renderers, and the
  texture filter, GPU Timing, Far Textures and GX Color on OpenGX, each alone
  and then in a few combinations (7 or 10 steps, 4 s each; in 240p no
  Bilinear steps, and OpenGX adds AA). Far Textures and GX Color change at
  once during the tour instead of at the next game start.
* GX Mask Bit (Settings > General > Advanced > GPU, GxMask): OpenGX keeps
  the PS1's mask bit (bit 15) per pixel, in the Wii's depth buffer. Games that
  read their picture back, blend their last frame or use the mask check get it
  as on a PS1; the picture itself is unchanged. ps1-tests gpu/mask-bit now
  passes under OpenGX. Fast is the 5.14.0 guess per 16x16 block.
* OpenGX: image loads (GP0 A0) obey the mask bits (E6), VRAM copies the
  mask check; a full-VRAM clear no longer leaves the next draw with the
  clear's matrices.
* Test runs: a chain runs up to 64 games (was 24), and its VRAM dumps are
  taken in the game (ChainDump picks the moment; OpenGX syncs first).

Ver 5.15.0 (2026-10-19):
* Save Settings > This Game now saves only the settings that differ from
  settings.ini. When the game starts, WiiStation reads settings.ini first and
  the game's own file on top, so a later change to settings.ini reaches every
  game except in the settings that game changed. A game's file never holds the
  language, the menu font, the game, save and state folders or devices, or the
  network share and its login; WiiStation ignores them in an older game file.
  Older full game files still load; the next This Game save makes them short.
* Load Settings > This Game reads settings.ini and then the game's file, as a
  game start does, and also takes an old .cfg game file.
* Filter Tour is Off at every start: no settings file saves or loads it.
* Test USB Pad moved to Settings > General > Advanced > Input ("USB Pad: Test").
  B on the test page goes back there.

* ANALOG button: with PSX Pad Type Analog, a key combination presses the
  DualShock's ANALOG button (digital <-> analog), as on a PlayStation: GameCube
  pad L+R+Start, HID pad L2+R2+Start, Classic L+R+ZR, Wii U Pro and GamePad
  L+R+ZR (or both sticks pressed in), Wiimote A+B+2. Set it in Configure
  Buttons (ANALOG, or Off). A note on screen says the new mode, or that the
  game locked it (Ape Escape). This replaces 5.14.0's rule that turned the pad
  analog by itself: MediEvil now starts digital, as on a PlayStation; press
  the combination once and the stick moves Dan.
Ver 5.14.0 (2026-10-18):
* Fixed: with the Soft renderers (the default), Deflicker and Deflicker Level
  did nothing in 480i (the usual mode): the game kept the menu's filter. OpenGX
  took them only after its first picture readback. They now apply at once.
* Fixed: Deflicker Level "Light" was no filter at all (the same as Deflicker
  off). Light now blurs a little; the new "Strong" blurs most and flickers least.
* Video, Advanced Graphics: new "Video Mode" (Auto, NTSC, PAL 50, PAL 60, 480p;
  applies at the next start; 480p falls back to Auto without a component cable)
  and "Filter Tour", which steps a running game through the texture filter,
  deflicker and trap filter settings and their combinations every 4 seconds,
  named under the FPS counter. The page's help notes are gone (SETTINGS.md).
* Trap filter: its help says it only works on composite and S-Video.
* Menu: L and R (- and + on a Wiimote, ZL and ZR on the Wii U GamePad) turn
  the tabs on every screen with tabs, the Advanced pages too. After the last
  tab comes the first; TabWrap = 0 in settings.ini stops them at the ends.
* Menu: closing a message (Credits, Quit, "Please load an ISO first") with B
  or OK leaves the cursor on the button you pressed. B on the main menu no
  longer moves the cursor.
* Settings, General: Load Settings (SD, USB, This Game) reads that settings
  file and applies it at once. Fast Load moved to Advanced, CD.
* Settings, Audio: "DSP Sound" is now "Sound Driver" (DSP or CPU). It picks the
  output driver only; the PS1 sound emulation is the same with either. The
  settings.ini key is SoundDriver; the old SoundHwAccel is still read. The
  same option is no longer repeated on Advanced, SPU.
* Settings: the Advanced buttons on General, Video and Audio are the same size;
  Audio's Sync Off and Rate buttons are as wide as the Sound Driver buttons.
* Configure Buttons: the GunCon, Justifier and mouse pictures no longer show
  garbage in their top-left corner, and are drawn at their own size.
* Fixed: the reverb's left-right reflections read the wrong place in the echo
  buffer (their own output, one buffer length earlier, instead of the other
  side's), so games and the BIOS with reverb on had a slightly different echo
  from a PlayStation's.
* Wii Remote lights: in the menu a remote shows its own number (remote 2 lights
  LED 2); in a game it shows the PlayStation player it plays. A remote that
  drops out and comes back gets its light again.
* Light guns and the mouse: a small crosshair shows where the Wii Remote points
  (both video plugins). GunReticle = 0 in settings.ini turns it off.
* Rumble: the DualShock's large motor now has its strength (the Wii motor runs
  for a share of each 8 frames), and every motor stops in the menu.
* Fixed: MediEvil and other games that leave analog mode to the ANALOG button
  stayed digital with PSX Pad Type Analog; the stick now moves Dan.
* Fixed: the mouse kept moving with the first movement it read (OpenGX).
* Auto Load Slot = Default now loads the built-in mapping at each game start;
  the previous game's mapping stayed before.

Ver 5.13.1 (2026-10-13):
* Fixed: after about 72 minutes of play (and every 72 minutes after that) the
  sound fell an eighth of a second behind the picture and played slightly high
  for about a minute while it caught up. The frame limiter's clock jumped back.
* Fixed (OpenGX): Gex 3's LOADING screen and Crash Bash's "SCEA Presents" logo
  were black; Crash Team Racing sat two lines too high with noise along the
  bottom; Ape Escape's intro left half of an old picture on screen.
* Fixed: saving a state could fail while playing a .chd, .pbp or .cbn image
  (memory ran short). The unpacking now uses the large memory, and save
  states do too.
* Compressed images: the read-ahead no longer delays the game's own reads.
* The Homebrew Channel agent inside WiiStation is version 1.10.4.
* Built with devkitPro's libogc 3 (faster SD and USB reading).

Ver 5.13.0 (2026-10-13):
* Faster: compressed images (.pbp, .cbn, .chd) are unpacked ahead, while the
  game waits for the next frame. On the Wii, Frogger spent 2.8-4.0 s of every
  30 s unpacking; now 0.2 s, less than a plain .bin. Uses the CD Read-Ahead
  option (on by default).
* Tip: .chd files made with "chdman createcd -c cdzs,cdfl" (zstd) are the same
  size and unpack faster than chdman's default.

Ver 5.12.3 (2026-10-13):
* Fixed: a .cbn image no longer fails to start when it is not the first game
  since power-on (it opened as an unreadable disc). Its index is now kept in
  the large memory, as .pbp and .chd indexes are.

Ver 5.12.2 (2026-10-13):
* Fixed: Spyro the Dragon shows its Sony and Universal logos with the OpenGX
  plugin (the screen stayed black). A picture the game loads while the screen
  is off, or changes without a page flip, is now shown.

Ver 5.12.1 (2026-10-12):
* Fixed: Gex 2 (and any game that streams CD audio while polling the drive)
  no longer stutters in its movies: the CD-XA sound sectors were delayed a
  little more each time, until the sound ran dry between them.
* Fixed: Gex 2's remote quest menu shows one cursor (it left every old one on
  screen). VRAM copies and image loads now draw black instead of leaving it out.
* Fixed: .cbn (CISO) images open (they never did on the Wii); .chd images with
  CD audio tracks play those tracks from the right place; a bare .iso or .bin
  boots. Every disc format was tested with the same game.

Ver 5.12.0 (2026-10-12):
* New: Advanced Graphics has Gamma (1.0 to 1.5, the TV picture) and Deflicker
  Level (Light: less blur than Normal). The page uses the new compact layout:
  two settings per line on one grid. Neither costs anything while a game runs.
* Changed: the PSX Pad Type buttons are as wide as their words (Standard,
  Analog, Stick D-pad), so none is cramped.

Ver 5.11.0 (2026-10-12):
* New: one menu framework draws and moves the focus on every page, so every
  page acts the same way. Back returns to the button you came from.
* New: screenshots. Choose a button combination per controller; Current ROM
  has a Screenshot button too.
* New: Settings > General > Advanced has a Memory page: free memory, texture
  pool, code buffer and PS1 buffers.
* New: button mappings have a Game slot (one file per game) and Auto Load
  Slot (now the default) loads it. A game without its own mapping gets the
  default mapping, not the previous game's.
* Changed: one Save on Configure Buttons writes every button file. The files
  have readable names in wiistation/buttons/ (gamecube.cfg, classic.cfg, ...;
  games/<game ID>.cfg). Old files are still read and never deleted.
* Changed: Video has one Frame Rate button: Auto, PS1, TV, Unlimited (was
  Limit FPS on Video and Frame Rate on Advanced). Old settings still load.
* New: a local achievement engine (rcheevos), off by default. No account or
  online awards yet. See SETTINGS.md.
* Fixed: unsaved menu settings are kept through a game load.
* Fixed: Configure Buttons' Slot button stopped at Game; it cycles again.
* Fixed: the Wiimote lights are set only when they change; idle timeout
  300 s, as in the Homebrew Channel.

Ver 5.10.0 (2026-10-11):
* New: Co-Op players' buttons turn on and off one by one. On Customize
  Co-Op, press A (or point) on any button or stick: the player gets or
  loses it. A set that is no preset is "Custom" and is kept in the
  settings (Coop1Buttons1 = "L1,Up,Cross,..."), per game too. The D-pad
  now reaches every button on that page.
* New: Quit writes sd:/wiistation/quit.log, one line per step on the way
  out, to find where a Wii froze on Quit.
* Test tools: "menukeys" presses menu buttons from an input script,
  "menupage 90" quits; settings given as arguments no longer autoboot.

Ver 5.9.6 (2026-10-09):
* New: Frame Rate (Advanced, GPU tab). Auto: 60 Hz (PAL 50) as before. TV: the
  game runs at the TV's own 59.94 Hz and every frame is shown on exactly one
  TV refresh, so motion is smoothest (Auto drops one frame about every 17
  seconds). PS1: a PlayStation's own 59.82 Hz (PAL 49.75), the exact speed.
* Changed: the Advanced tabs are in the same places and sizes as the
  Settings tabs, and both rows of tabs moved up a little. The rows below
  them start where the Settings rows start.
* Changed: the SPU tab is about the sound plugin: which plugin it is and
  whether the CPU or the Wii's DSP mixes its output. The sound quality
  options are on Audio, Advanced again.

Ver 5.9.5 (2026-10-09):
* New: Settings > General > Advanced (was Plugins) has tabs across the top:
  CPU, GPU, SPU, CD and Input. The CD settings moved into its CD tab, and the
  sound settings of Audio > Advanced are its SPU tab.
* New: CPU Speed (Advanced, CPU tab): 50% to 300%, or Auto (normal, with the
  fixes some games need). Over 100% can make a game that slows down smoother,
  but uses more of the Wii's CPU, and some games break. Use Save Settings,
  This Game to keep it for one game only.
* New: Port Timing (Advanced, Input tab), which was only in the settings file.
* Changed: the frame counter (Show FPS) shows what really happens, once a
  second: FPS = frames the game drew, VPS = emulated screen refreshes, and the
  speed in %. It used to show the refresh rate, held at 59.83 whenever it was
  higher.

Ver 5.9.4 (2026-10-09):
* Fixed: Gex 3 - Deep Cover Gecko crashed two seconds after it started (with
  the built-in BIOS). With the built-in BIOS, the emulated CD drive could
  deliver sectors far too fast: after a game was late to take one sector, the
  drive's timer ran ahead and fired again at almost every BIOS call. Gex 3's
  CD code was flooded and broke its own memory.
* Fixed: Gex 2 - Enter the Gecko ran slowly on a Wii (0.7x): the same CD
  timer bug read 20,000 sectors a second in bursts. Other games that stream
  from the disc with the built-in BIOS can run faster too.
* Faster: games in CloneCD format (.ccd/.img/.sub) read the .sub file with a
  small buffer: each position query read 16 KB from the card before.

Ver 5.9.3 (2026-10-09):
* Fixed: Crash Team Racing crashed at the end of a race (seen at the end of a
  2-player Grand Prix). When the recompiler's code memory was full, a piece of
  game code could run twice, which broke the game's data. A full code memory
  now never runs code twice.
* Fixed: lag spikes of 0.2-0.4 seconds during play (Crash Team Racing). When
  the code memory was full, the recompiler threw away code at random, also
  code the game was using, and had to compile it again at once. It now throws
  away the oldest code first, and the code memory is larger (2 MB, was
  1.5 MB): in a 10-minute Crash Team Racing run on a Wii it did not fill.
* Fixed: a game in CloneCD format (.ccd/.img/.sub) had a wrong disc length
  (4 seconds) in its table of contents.

Ver 5.9.2 (2026-10-09):
* Fixed: an automated run (a chain of games from autoboot.txt) stopped at the
  first error message, waiting for an OK nobody pressed -- 8 hours on the test
  Wii, behind "This disc cannot be read". During a chain, messages now go to
  the logs and the chain goes on.
* Debug builds keep a heartbeat (sd:/wiistation/hb.log): every 2 seconds what
  WiiStation is doing, and any call to the Wii's system that has not
  returned, so a freeze says where it is.

Ver 5.9.1 (2026-10-09):
* Load from DVD no longer freezes the Wii on a disc its drive cannot read.
  The drive's spin-up could run on for minutes (or until the disc was taken
  out), and every other request to the Wii's system, the SD card's included,
  waited behind it. Now a spin-up gets 20 seconds, then WiiStation stops the
  drive's command and resets the drive, and says the disc cannot be read.
* Disc reads have a time limit and let the rest of WiiStation run while they
  wait; a read that fails says the drive's error code.
* A Wii whose drive is too new to read DVDs (made after mid-2008) is told so,
  and no disc is spun up.

Ver 5.9.0 (2026-10-08):
* Load from DVD works. It listed a disc but no game ever started from it: the
  browser kept only the file's name, which the CD reader could not open. The
  disc is now mounted as "dvd:" and read like the SD card, so .cue/.bin with
  CD audio tracks, CHD and PBP images all work from a DVD.
* Discs burned with Windows (Explorer's "Burn to disc") are read: the disc
  library looped on their folder sizes and found no file at all.
* Load from DVD says what is wrong: no disc, a disc it cannot read (a CD, or
  a DVD-R in a drive that cannot read one), or no access to the drive.
* A disc changed in the drive is read the next time Load from DVD opens.

Ver 5.8.0 (2026-10-08):
* Games on a network share run much faster. WiiStation reads the share in 16 KB
  requests on a thread of its own, and asks for the next 16 KB while the game
  uses the last. Ape Escape's first 20 seconds on the test Wii: 0.97x (as fast
  as from the SD card), where 5.7.0 ran at 0.80x and waited 8 s for the network.
* The share uses 256 KB of memory for its read buffers instead of 1 MB.

Ver 5.7.2 (2026-10-08):
* Fixed: the spinning logo was gone from every menu page since 5.4.0. Configure
  Input hid it while the menu was being built, before it was ever shown.

Ver 5.7.1 (2026-10-08):
* Fixed: a game loaded after another one could stop at its first frame
  ("Lightrec: fault 6") when memory had been used in between, e.g. by two
  games from the network share in a row. The recompiler now keeps its 2.5 MB
  of state from game to game instead of asking for it again each time.
* If the recompiler ever cannot start, WiiStation now stops with "Lightrec: no
  memory for its state" instead of running the game on nothing.

Ver 5.7.0 (2026-10-08):
* Network shares work with current servers: SMB2 and SMB3 through libsmb2,
  in place of a library that spoke only SMB1 (off by default on Samba 4.11+,
  Windows 10 and 11). No password length limit; NTLMv2 logins.
* RomDirSMB: the game folders on the share, as RomDirSD (folders inside the
  share open like folders on the SD card).
* Games on the share read in small pieces after a jump and larger ones while
  they read on, and get no CD read-ahead: the Wii's Wi-Fi gives about
  350 KB/s. Expect waits in loading screens and videos; CHD images help.
* A read that fails reconnects to the share once, so a short network drop
  does not stop the game.
* "Cannot connect to the share" says the server's reason.

Ver 5.6.1 (2026-10-08):
* The settings file is wiistation/settings.ini (was settingsRX2022.cfg), and
  a game's own settings are wiistation/settings/<serial>.ini (was .cfg). The
  lines are the same; rename your file to keep it. Any INI editor can open it:
  ; comments, indented lines and [section] lines are now accepted.
* settings.ini is written in sections ([General], [Video], [Audio], ...), each
  with a short comment. example_settings.ini shows every setting, its default
  and what it does.
* RomDirSD and RomDirUSB replace RomDir1..RomDir4: a list of game folders with
  a comma between two folders, e.g. RomDirSD = "/wiistation/isos, /roms/psx".

Ver 5.6.0 (2026-10-07):
* WiiStation's folder on the SD card and USB device is now /wiistation (was
  /wiisxrx): settings, saves, save states, BIOS, fonts, languages, controller
  files and logs. Nothing is moved for you: to keep your settings, saves and
  BIOS, move or copy them from /wiisxrx to /wiistation.
* Games can be anywhere: RomDir1..RomDir4 in settingsRX2022.cfg name up to four
  folders (default sd:/wiistation/isos and usb:/wiistation/isos). Load from
  SD/USB opens the one that exists, or lists them when there are several.
  The card is FAT, which has no links or junctions; this replaces them.
* Saving settings creates /wiistation when the card does not have it yet.
* `hbc.py lastlog` shows WiiStation's output after it exits: it now leaves
  with exit(), which also lets the HBC agent write the SD card back.

Ver 5.5.0 (2026-10-07):
* The Homebrew Channel agent from HBC 1.10.0 (was 1.9.3). With HBC 1.10 on the
  Wii, `hbc.py status` now shows WiiStation's frame pacing and main stack use,
  a stack overflow, assert() or abort() is reported like a crash, and the SD
  card is written back when WiiStation exits. Reset still returns to the menu.

Ver 5.4.3 (2026-10-05):
* Configure Buttons says "PSX Port 1" where it said "PSX Pad 1", as
  Configure Input does.

Ver 5.4.2 (2026-10-05):
* Configure Buttons: each mapping button grows to fit its label ("Start+Z",
  "D-Down", "RS-Button" ran past their buttons), without touching its
  neighbors; the stacked buttons no longer overlap.
* Buttons that size themselves to their label leave a few more pixels
  around it.

Ver 5.4.1 (2026-10-04):
* Configure Buttons works with a Co-Op or Multitap port: it maps each
  player's or slot's controller in turn (Next), where it showed "No Physical
  Controller Assigned" for the port.

Ver 5.4.0 (2026-10-04):
* Co-Op: up to 8 controllers play one PlayStation port together, so more
  people can play a game made for fewer. Each player gets a layout (whole
  pad, left or right half, D-pad and sticks, action or shoulder buttons):
  Settings > Input > Configure Input, Manual, port type Co-Op, Customize.
* Configure Input laid out like the Settings tabs: "PSX Port 1/2" at the top
  of each port's column; the Multitap and Co-Op rows appear only when chosen.
* Switching Configure Input to Automatic turns a Multitap or Co-Op port back
  into a plain port (a port left as Multitap could not be seen or changed).
* Wii Remote LEDs show the player number on multitap slots and Co-Op players
  (they went dark from the third slot on).
* Settings > Input: the Controller Type buttons line up and "Stick D-pad"
  fits its button.
* A game loaded after another one starts with all of VRAM clear; the last
  few pixels of the last row kept the previous game's data.

Ver 5.3.0 (2026-10-02):
* New controller type "Stick D-pad": a digital pad whose D-pad the left stick
  also presses (8 directions), for games that read only the D-pad.
* Multitap: slots A-D start as GameCube pads 1-4, and automatic assignment
  keeps each GameCube pad in its own slot (players no longer move around when
  a pad answers late, and port 2 keeps its pad).
* Changing a port's controller type while a game runs (for example Multitap to
  GameCube pad) now works: the port is empty for half a second, as with a real
  swap, so the game finds the new controller.
* Crash Bash: the pause menu and the rules screens show their text in OpenGX
  and Soft Fast (the GPU list is walked in slices, as the console does).
* Soft Timed: the frame limiter no longer waits when the game is already late
  (Crash Bash on the Wii: 0.91x -> 1.00x).

Ver 5.2.0 (2026-10-01):
* Lightrec core synced to upstream a7464cc. On a real Wii the emulated CPU
  costs 0.3-0.8 points less load in each of 8 test games. On the PS1 test
  programs (ps1-tests) its results are the same as the old core's.
* PS-X EXE programs run on Lightrec (the CPU core was never made for them).
* Fixed two emulator crashes on unusual I/O accesses (unaligned words to
  hardware registers, 8/16-bit cache-control accesses).
* HLE BIOS printf: field flags (%-10s) and signed %d.
* Docs/ACCURACY.md: measured differences from a real PS1, and the plan for
  a Fast and an Accurate option.

Ver 5.1.0 (2026-10-01):
* VRAM fills 3-4x faster on the Wii; CPU-to-VRAM transfers copy words
  (FF7 load 65.3 -> 53.9%; mean of 8 games 38.9 -> 35.8%).
* FMV: 24-bit tiles and MDEC color clamps faster; output unchanged.
* XA audio filter 36% cheaper; Lightrec map lookup cached.
* CD read-ahead on by default. New setting LimiterDebt (125 ms by default).
* Crash reports for hangs, guest crashes, missing audio and exceptions
  (HBC agent 1.9.3).

Ver Beta 4.5:
* Added OpenGx hardware rendering plugin.
  The original code uses GLES from pcsxRearm,
  and the OpenGx framework comes from [https://github.com/davidgfnet/opengx].
  Key fixes for GLES and OpenGx:
    Little-endian to big-endian conversion.
    Display of opaque pixels in transparent mode.
    Sub-texture pixel updates.
    Other minor fixes.
  Currently known major issues:
    Due to texture caching, sometimes cached textures are not cleared in time, resulting in incorrect rendering on the screen.
    Data transferred via the primLoadImage command occasionally fails to load correctly onto the screen.

Ver Beta 4.0:
* HID controllers support.
  (The PS2 and PS3 controllers (includes vibration) has been fully tested.)
* Using the speed limit logic of old video plugins.
* Merged some code from PCSX-ReARMed to reduce the occurrence/happening of some issues.
* Reduced the use of MEM1 memory and fixed crashes/errors in games such as Rayman 2.
* Added Gaussian interpolation to SPU as an option.
* Add the old software rendering method as the default GPU plugin. The new one
  will be kept as an option in case some games don't work correctly there.
* Update libCHDr to latest until git 07a564a.
* Update Lightrec to latest until git 8d3db1a.

Ver 3.2:
* SPU: Correction for overflow of 16 digit numbers.
* psxbios: implement yet more memcard details.
* Fixes Formula 1 freezing at first loading screen.
* Update to latest libCHDr.
* To avoid errors where CHD games occupy too much memory, move Lightrec's 4M Buffer to MEM2 memory.

Ver 3.0:
* 240p/Lightgun/Mouse/Multitap support.
* CHD v1-v5 compressed format support.
* Add Force NTSC mode.
* Rewritten PCM decoding logic using the paired single instruction,
  there is a slight increase in corresponding speed.
* Imported the latest SPU, GPU, BIOS, Cdrom and other modules from PCSX ReARMed.
* Add separate save function, each game can save its own settings.
* Add old dynamic compilation core, For games where the Lightrec core cannot run,
  you can try the old dynamic compilation core.
* Many other minor corrections.

Ver 2.4:
* Use devkitPPC r41-2 + libOGC2 to compile code and solve the problem of some games not running.
* Use the latest Lightrec to solve the problem of slow of Destruction Derby Raw.
* Fix the logic of disc swapping, and games such as Metal Gear Solid can be swapped normally.
* Reduce loading time for special games.
  Now limited to games Parasite Eve and LEGO Island 2 only, Perhaps it should be an option.
* Fix the logic of CDDA playback, no longer using multithreading.
* Fix the issue of Vib Ribbon playing Reverb sound, there are still minor issues.

Ver 2.0:
* Use a new dynamic compilation (dynarec) method Lightrec, a super cool way.
  Many games can now run at full speed.
* Add Wii Message Board support for write playlog (Thanks SuperrSonic and saulfabregwiivc).
* Import the GTE code of PCSX-ReARMed.
* Fix issues
    Batman Beyond: Return of the Joker can't be played.
    Driver 2 code dump error on mission "Chase the Gunman".
* Modify the code to support higher versions of devkitPPC.
  ※※※ Note: For issue 81, the latest high version of libOGC must be used.

Ver 1.0:
* Incorporating the CDROM and CDRISO codes of PCSX-ReARMed.
* CDDA (Compact Disc Digital Audio) tracks support.
* Incorporating the timer codes of PCSX-ReARMed.
* Combined the DFSound module of PCSX-ReARMed and used the SDL library.
* Modification of some dynamic compilation instructions.
* Support for multiple languages.
  ※※※ Note: It reads a font file in a fixed location, so make sure that [sd:/wiistation/fonts/chs.dat] exists ※※※
* For some customed Chinese culture games, specific BIOS is automatically loaded.
* Other minor corrections, such as disc changing (swap) and automatic fixes (autoFix functions) for some games.

